title: ActiveRunnerP on Hardware

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/ActiveRunnerP.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex01_HelloP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-06-01.png
  - .assets/img-102-06-02.png
  - .assets/img-102-01-03.png
steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Running on Real HW}

      We've now visited all of the supporting modules called directly&thinsp;/&thinsp;indirectly by the {[cu]ActiveRunnerP} program.

      Before visiting {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} in next tour, let's take a giant step forward in your mastery of **EMS**:

      <div class="em-info">
      
      Let's execute {[cu]ActiveRunner} on <u>real</u> hardware&thinsp;!!

      </div>

      Until now, we've used the `rpi.2040://PI_PICO-sim$$` board as our target; review {[tr,102/01/05]} for details. 

      But the **EMS** code you've studied along the **CoreMark** [and **Basic Examples**] tours in fact remains 100% portable.

      <div class="em-info">

      Skim this tour while awaiting your board's arrival⏳.

      </div>

      So let's give it a go&thinsp;&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,developer_board]&thinsp;Choose Your Board}

      We've already pointed out the <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>&thinsp;{[em]Board} icon {[dc,1]} found in the **Status Bar** at the bottom of your **VS Code** window.

      Clicking {[dc,1]} reveals a menu {[dc,2]} listing all target boards sup&shy;ported by **EMS distros** present within the **EM&bull;porium**.

      Make your selection and then watch the <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>&thinsp;{[em]Board} change:

      {[DC,3]} Nordic's [**NRF52_DK**](https://www.nordicsemi.com/Products/Development-hardware/nRF52-DK) board<br>
      {[DC,4]} Nordic's [**NRF54L15_DK**](https://www.nordicsemi.com/Products/Development-hardware/nRF54L15-DK) board<br>
      {[DC,5]} TI's [**LP_EM_CC2340R5**](https://www.ti.com/tool/LP-EM-CC2340R5) board<br>

      You'll also see a picture of the selected board as further confirmation of your choice.

      <div class="em-info">

      Future tours explain the labeling within these pictures.

      </div>
      

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    acts: [$build]
    text: |
      {[ht,detector_smoke]&thinsp;%[cu]BlinkerP% Smoke Test}

      ☑️&thinsp; First, verify you've chosen a supported {[em]Board}.

      ☑️&thinsp; Next, verify you've plugged *real* HW into your PC.

      ☑️&thinsp; Finally, click the {[cb,build]} button and see what happens next.

      <div class="em-info">

      The exact location of `AppLed` varies across boards; and some boards show the &thinsp;[RED]&thinsp;`SysLed` as &thinsp;[GREEN].

      </div>

      If successful, you'll have an experience not unlike the first {[cu]BlinkerP} run {[tr,100/01/04]} on **Pi Pico** simulator.&thinsp; And if not&thinsp;&hellip;

      ❓&thinsp;Review the three ☑️ items above &ndash; just in case&thinsp;&hellip;

      ❓&thinsp;**Build** success / **Load** failure {[em]⇒} HW connection issues.

      ❓&thinsp;Ask for help on the **EM&bull;porium** [Discussions](https://github.com/em-foundation/emporium/discussions) page.

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,cable]&thinsp;Setup the Serial Monitor}

  - cmds:
      - open 3
    acts: [$build]
    text: |
      {[ht,hand_gesture]&thinsp;Say %[cu]HelloP%}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    acts: [$build]
    text: |
      {[ht,celebration]&thinsp;And Now&thinsp;&hellip;}

  - cmds:
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} blah.

      {[bi,task_alt]} blah.

      {[bi,task_alt]} blah.

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      {[hc]}
